  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 AM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered to locally numerous showers and \nstorms will be possible this afternoon/evening and be capable of\nstrong to severe straight-line wind gusts and locally heavy \nrainfall amounts of > 1\".\n\nA pair of fronts are expected the shift across our area over \nthe next 24 to 36 hours. The lead boundary will be a pre-frontal\ntrough, currently stretching roughly from Richmond to Charlotte\nin the 05z obs, which will mix southeast throughout the morning\nand bring atypical hot and humid conditions within northerly \nflow across the forecast area. Trailing this boundary will be \nthe synoptic cold front, which will likely be working through \nthe Virginias and southern Ohio Valley through the daylight \nhours.\n\nIn between these two boundaries, surface dew points should \nremain in the mid/upper 60s to low 70s throughout peak heating \nas temperatures rise well into the 90s. This should provide a \nmoderately unstable air mass with instability ranging from \n1000-2000 J/kg. Although isolated pulse air mass showers/storms \ncan't be ruled out anywhere through the afternoon (with \nconvective temps in the low 90s), the greatest convective \ncoverage will likely be along a the track of upstream showers \nand storms currently working through Ohio as of 06z. Mean-wind \nsteering flow should continue to track this cluster towards the \ncentral Appalachians by early afternoon and then track generally\nsouth and into central NC during peak heating. Poor mid-lvl \nlapse rates and related marginal instability should preclude a \nmore widespread severe threat, however, steep low-lvl lapse \nrates in excess of 9 C/km over the Piedmont could favor storms \norganizing along a common cold-pool and result in localized \nstrong to severe straight-line wind gusts, despite weak \ntropospheric wind shear (5-10 kts). \n\nThe synoptic cold front should start to working into the area \nfrom the north around or shortly after midnight and may support \na continuation of shallow showers through the overnight period.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Monday into Tuesday will likely feature \nscattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms. Beneficial rain\npossible, remaining hot Monday. Turning cooler later Tuesday, \nespecially over north-central areas.\n\nModels continue to suggest a potentially moist period for our \nregion Monday into Tuesday. This is due to the mid/upper trough \napproaching from the Midwest and Ohio Valley. One cold front is \nexpected to reach NC Monday afternoon and possibly stall, with \nanother cold front expected to backdoor the region late Tuesday \ninto Tuesday night. There should be a couple of rounds of \nshowers/storms, especially during the afternoon and evening \nMonday into Tuesday night. Some beneficial rainfall is expected,\nbut as is typical with thunderstorms some areas will get the \nbeneficial rainfall of 1 to locally 2 inches, while some others \nmay be left out. Much more on this forecast through the weekend \nupdates. It will likely continue very warm and humid Monday, \nwith some cooler conditions Tuesday across the north (due to \nclouds/showers/storms). Monday highs should be in the mid 80s to\nlower 90s south. Highs Tuesday will be around 80 north to near \n90 south. Lows in the mid 60s to around 70. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A NE flow and cold air damming will yield much\ncooler and cloudier conditions, possibly accompanied by periods\nof light rain and drizzle, Tuesday night into Thursday.\n\nOnce the cold front settles south of the region later Tuesday \nand Tuesday night, a low level NE flow will continue to feed low\nlevel moisture into the region. This would bring stable \nconditions and would mean that stratus, fog, and periods of \nlight rain/drizzle or light showers will be likely Tuesday night\ninto Wednesday and Thursday. The combination of the NE flow, \nmoisture, cloudiness, and some light rain will keep daily highs \nin the 70s (or much below normal for the first time in weeks). \nLows in the 60s, with some 50s possible north-central Wed-Fri.",
